kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
: styri@balder.nta.no (YuNoHoo) writes:
: >There's a lot of material used in academic research that isn't public,
: >material that must be checked out.
: 
: Is there? Are Degas prints part of this restricted material?

Good grief, Carl manages to keep up the arguing even at the point we
probably agree. Taking our minds off the wonderful world of Degas the
point is that some of the material collected through research would be
unethical and probably illegal to publish. Sometimes a university do
contract work for some party that doesn't want the results disclosed.
(You may of course argue that universities shouldn't involve in that...)

Degas prints are probably not in this category. A private art collection
may be in this category.

[...]
: >Academic research demands a certain level of honesty. It really bothers
: >me when people aren't able to say what is leisure and general interest,
: >and what is study and research.
: [...]
: 
: I think academics should be able to view Degas on-line (in a
: nonharassing way) even if it is just for leisure and general interest.
: I don't think students always know (or can be expected to know) what
: they are doing for "general interest" and what they are doing for
: "study".

I agree it's not always a sharp line between work and leisure, and what
is work for one student may be just leisure for another. It is, however,
a good thing for students to develop a good judgement for what work is
productive and what work is futile.

A university should provide an environment for leisure activities as well
as work and study. But, it's stupid to demand that resources intended for
work and study should be equally available for leisure. The real problem
is of course that grey area were some feel the use of resources is
justified and others don't.

It's no big issue to view Degas on-line if you ignore the resource issue.
Copyrighted work, however would be quite another thing.

