Well, if UiB runs this server, then fine with me. I just think they have
the right to remove it if they want, and if they think it is misuse of their
resources.

Just like I think any organisation is free to remove a WWW server if they 
don't want to fund it and use the resources for something else.

Lets put this in an extreme perspective. Students at University XXXXX makes
this increadibly well done index of pictures of character that can without
doubt be said to be pornography (and lets add.. it is pictures that withouth
any doubt has no other function than leisure). Well, in the true spirit of 
Internet use, this homepage gets very heavily accessed, until the day comes 
when it renders the $70000 workstation the WWW server runs on and the network 
to which it is connected useless for more or less anything serving horny 
netters (which always seems to have loads of time too wait for anything
to get across even the worst links...).

Now.. under the right of your academic freedom, the CS section or the 
University administration can do nothing about it, and is forced to go to the 
government for money to fund a better network link, and a new workstation so 
that it is able to continue it's role in a modern society.

What do you think the reaction will be?
What do you think taxpayers will say when they read in leadig newspapers that 
equipment worth 3-4 years income for an average industrial worker should be 
used this way? Not to mentione when some caclulates how many that could be 
brought out of unemployment if these money where used for something else.

My opinion is that the University don't need to have editorial control and it 
probably don't have such a thing either. It only has a right to do something 
about misuse of their resources if they want too. In the rules you sign here 
when you get an account, there is some paragraphs that concerns use which 
isn't related to studies. I believe this is an issue that is covered by these 
rules.

As I have said before, I support freedom of speach, however, I don't support 
your academic freedom of using taxmoney as you like. In a way, there really 
never was freedom in this case, only responsibility.

