terjem@stud.cs.uit.no (Terje Normann Marthinussen) writes:

[....]
>Now.. under the right of your academic freedom, the CS section or the
>University administration can do nothing about it [an over popular
>porn page -cmk], and is forced to go to the government for money to
>fund a better network link, and a new workstation so that it is able
>to continue it's role in a modern society.

I'm sorry if I was unclear. This is not my position.

My position is:

1. The school can ban illegal material.

2. The school limit the resources it provides to support pages. (So,
if some pages take too much computer and network
resources, the school can restrict them.)

3. The school can give more resources to material that is more
valuable to university mission. (So, if it thinks the Veronica server
is worth a whole machine, but that no recreational home page should
take more than 1% of the computer or network capacity, that would be
fine.)

[...]
>In the rules you sign here when you get an account, there is some
>paragraphs that concerns use which isn't related to studies. I
>believe this is an issue that is covered by these rules.
[...]

Let me create a scenario at the other extreme:

  1. Student #1 creates a World Cup football/soccer page with new
     results constantly being updated. The page is very popular and
     people all over the world read and keep re-reading it (because
     they want to see the updates). Supporting this page takes
     10% of the university's network capacity.

  2. Student #2 creates a Norwegian Dr. Fun cartoon homepage. It is
     read once a day by a dozen people, half on campus. It probably
     saves more network resources that it uses because people don't
     access the main Dr. Fun homepage in the U.S. as much. In anycase,
     it uses .0001% of the university's network capacity. Some
     Dr. Fun cartoons offend some people, but none are illegal.

I think it would be a violation of academic freedom for the university
to ban Dr. Fun and not the more expensive World Cup page.

In other words, if a university allows personal recreational pages, it
is OK to regulate them on the basis of cost, but not on the basis of
offensiveness.

- Carl

