============= begining of repost ========
>From marek@mcs.com Mon May  9 20:01:00 1994

Carl,

Carl, you say you focused on NU student policies vs. ACNS policy, and
you ask about staff-only policies, but there appear no staff-only
policies, other than the ACNS policies on line.  I have looked
exhaustively for any and all policies.  To be found was only the ACNS
on-line material referring to "users", which would appear to include
the entire NU community, from students to staff to faculty to visiting
researchers to courtesy users (assuming there are such, say, visiting
speakers checking their e-mail).

I have read on nwu.general a remark that ACNS has been
writing/rewriting a policy for some time now, for what it's worth.

Based on the language then a part of the ACNS policy pertaining to
harassment by users, I made my complaint against Donald Davis as user,
not as a student, of ACNS, which to the best of my knowledge, much of
it hearsay, precipitated the whole upheaval:

Apparently, ACNS found out, while processing my complaint, from the NU
General Counsel that nearly all of the ACNS use policy with respect to
speech/harassment is unenforcable.  This was followed by the adoption
by ACNS of an ad hoc policy mandating disabling the access of users
who are excessively consuming resources, where apparently being a
subject of a received complaint, any complaint, constitutes consuming
resources excessively, never mind the merit of the complaint, and
never mind showing to and discussing the actual complaint with the user.

What I don't understand is why the ACNS postmaster and the ACNS sys
admin both advertised for complaints in public on the net in the first
place.  Or why the sys administorator at ACNS in a letter to an off-site
user claimed inability to control the content of ACNS users mail,
and that ACNS is committed to free speech, as well as urging "all
involved" to peacably settle their differences, only to telephone me
days later iforming me of the disabling of my access at ACNS.

To my knowledge, this policy, ostensibly applicable to all users, has
been used against 2 users already (Donald Davis, and me, 2 weeks
apart), and has yet to be announced to the general NU community:
I monitor nwu.gneral and nwu.comp.announce and no such traffic has come up.

My second to biggest problem with this policy is that it will cause
sites adopting it to become flooded in escalating complaints,
nealry all bogus from the standpoint of that system's policies.

But my biggest problem with this policy is its vulnerability to
malicious and spurious complaints which can result in revoking access
as a form of yielding to pressure from the outside or to rid oneself
of one's responsibilities as a sys admin.  What kind of system admin
would get rid of a user rather than get rid of a complaint, esp. if
the complaint is bogus?  What kind of director of systems would do that?

In particular, this policy does not allow any sort of defense from
persecution (sic) since anyone can write to any system administration
or bother the director of computing by phone (this has actually
happened at the University of Utah recently in connection with a Utah
user and a Columbus, Ohio complaining party; for other examples of
recent requests for account-yanking, see rec.arts.poems), and if the
policy of the sys admin is to knee-jerk take away access without
evaluating the merit of the complaint or, better yet, screening it for
belonging under the scope of the system policies -- what kind of fair
treatment is that?

Here is my solution to the complaint-mail-bomb problem:

If a complaint has nothing to do with the system policies, it should
be returned with a polite note to that effect and perhaps an on-line
copy of the policies.  This takes minimum fuss, a keystroke, and
leaves the organization with the task of drafting reasonable system
policies, ones, for example, that do not set it up as an arbiter of
"tone of postings" (my crime, the reason for my ACNS access denial,
still in effect since mid-April), and it off-loads any "excessive
resource consumption".

The alternative is bad press, bad reputation, possibly litigation and
the tarnishing of professional reputations of the organization, the
image of the university as promoting academic freedom, and injury and
expense to the individuals involved.

The price of organizational heavy-handedness and overacting can be far
greater than the price of hiring appropriate help (BA in English) to
do the job right and put the policies in place, the kind that are not
overturned by the legal dept after months of lying around.  Basically,
a computing org defers the arbitration of misdeeds to the University
mechanisms already set up for this purpose and concerns itself only
with the health of its machinery, circuitry and fair access to all, as
well as privacy.  Black and white stuff, no rocket science.

It is beyond me why ACNS has taken years to get to its present state of
acute disrepair in policy-making, given all the writing and technical
talent available on campus, not to forget one of the best Law faculties
in the nation who surely could be tapped for help if informally. :)

Carl, back to student vs. user at ACNS and NU:  I think you did not
focus on students exactly, since you did comment on sections of ACNS
policy concerning "users" or "a user", and those characterizations
pertain to staff, faculty, visiting researchers as well as students.

You interchange the terms user and student, just as ACNS addresses
only users but there exist separate student policies as well as ACNS
policies.

Under what you quoted, ACNS is treating me at conflict with its own policy
on speech, since they suspended my account because of complaints pertaining
to my Usenet traffic.

I think you left a job half-finished.  You blurred the distinction
that is really crucial -- user vs. student -- and even your farewell
summation implied that ACNS' policy for students was a-okay, one of
the best, even though you were really refering to Northwestern
University's student policy as cited on the NU gopher, not ACNS's
policies at all, which you admit, are deeply flawed, and at this
point, in disarray even.  Remember, you announced in your first
sentence that you are reviewing ACNS's policy (not ACNS policy for
students).  Do you see my point?

Honestly, I don't know where to find a staff-only policy.  I assume
that the Human Resources employee handbook is what you mean, but it
does not deal with matters of ACNS machine usage or Usenet academic
freedom, or any academic freedom.  It says how many coffee breaks I
can have. :) :) :).  (I don't take any, thus have saved the University
millions and millions of dollars...)

I think you should take ACNS at its published word, and consider their
policies to be ACNS policies for all NU community; they certainly did
not differentiate in the treatment of Donald Davis and me, in fact,
they blurred all possible distinctions and suspended both accesses,
albeit his 2 weeks earlier and perhaps from a different cause, but I
am not privy to ACNS complaint file, which, come to think of it should
be open to the public, to protect the accused, after all the
irrelevant complaints are sumamrily tossed out by the postmaster,
unseen by anyone.  Only well-conceived of policy delivers such lucid,
inexpensive disposition of traffic, which is what we all want, I wager.

If complaining was accountable, the quality of it would increase
and the nuissance of it would evaporate.

				-- Marek Lugowski
