sense.  (In fact, we even have separate Novell PC servers for our
students and our administrative staff.)

Now, if your Registrar, Business, and Student Affairs offices are using 
the same box as X hundred/thousand students, *then* there's a distinction 
to be made.  How many of us are in that situation?

I'd also question any computer usage policy that differentiated be-
tween student use and administrative use when talking about 'disruptive'
activity.  My system doesn't know if 'morgan' is a freshman, faculty
member or the High Janitor - and my policy shouldn't care, either.
Can anyone give an example of a 'disruptive' act that should result
in disciplinary action against a student, but no action against any-
one else?

For the purposes of our policies, users should be users.  In an ideal
environment, our only recognition of the 'subclasses' of users would be 
in our definition of the 'appropriate authority' to whom problems would
be directed:

	Student user: Dean of Students
	Faculty user: Department Chairmen or Dean of college
	Staff user:   Supervisor or Department Chairman

Remember, folks, we shouldn't judge; our task is to forward matters,
as necessary, to those who do.   We, as admins, should have the op-
tion of NOT filing charges/pursuing action, but we should NOT be the
ones determining sanctions.

