The only network for which I could find this information was NSFNet,
one of the larger backbones in the USA.  Based on their statistics,
the percentage has actually stayed relatively constant from January
1991 to the present; news transport via NNTP accounts for 8-10 per-
cent of total backbone traffic each month.

It should be noted, however, that these statistics reflect *only*
that traffic which crossed the NSFNET backbone; it does not, in 
any way, reflect the situation on your local or regional network.
I don't think you can argue that these statistics apply to AARNet.

You might want to run NNStat.  This public domain program is the
one used to generate the monthly traffic reports for NSFNet.  It
can be configured to report on almost any characteristic of your
network traffic.  It may be retrieved from ftp.merit.edu; look in
the directory "/pub/NNStat".  (It is a Unix program, and it re-
quires root privileges to run.)

