In article <CpI7tD.Fp2@cs.uiuc.edu>, Carl M Kadie <kadie@cs.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>chambles@whale.st.usm.edu (John William Chambless) writes:
>>Yes, they have a job to do, and as professionals, they also have
>>certain rights, including the right to exclude from their system
>>users who become major nuisances.That's  not just a right; it's 
>>part of the administrator's JOB.
>
>But as far as punishing a student for intermittently posting commerical 
>articles to Usenet, I don't think any university sys admin should have 
>such authority. At a state university, from many types of accounts, such 
>authority would probably be unconstitutional.

Well, I think we need to define "commercial" a bit more clearly.

Most of us, I suspect, would have no problem with the occasional
"selling my old TV" or "computer parts for sale/trade" message;
to me, that falls into the acceptable category, since it is not
an *ongoing* for-profit enterprise.

When we start seeing messages like "I'm selling magazine subscrip-
tions" or "Comic Book Warehouse now online at foo.edu," I think
we've crossed a line.  We've gone from a one-shot "selling my TV"
to an *ongoing* for-profit enterprise.  At that point, the user has
also crossed a line; they've exchanged their status as a student
(or faculty, or staff) for that of a businessman.  Should we ex-
pect to operate a business *and* enjoy academic protections at
the same time?  I think not.

Many universities - especially state universities - have explicit
policies regarding commercial use of their computing facilities.
At this university, for example, our policy states:

	The user may not use computing resources for any commercial
	purpose without prior written authorization from the Vice
	President for Information Systems, a designee, or the ap-
	propriate system administrator.

Since I fall into the latter category, I feel that the policy I
outlined above is a fair compromise.  Quite frankly, I don't be-
lieve that Joe Student (or Jane Faculty, or Bill Staffmember) 
should have a 'right' to conduct personal, *ongoing*, for-profit 
business with our resources.  

I will readily admit that the line I've drawn can be vague; however,
the very nature of this issue makes the implementation of more spe-
cific rules extremely difficult.  We don't want to throw the baby
(the occasional "selling my TV" posting) out with the bathwater 
(the "I'm setting up my CD distributorship on fing.edu" posting).

In the particular case at hand, I will say that any suspension, or
other sanction, should be approved by the appropriate authority.  I
don't see how this could be classified as an emergency sanction (it's
only Usenet, folks), so I would hope that the admins cleared the ac-
tion with the proper authority.  

--Wes

