morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:

]When we start seeing messages like "I'm selling magazine subscrip-
]tions" or "Comic Book Warehouse now online at foo.edu," I think
]we've crossed a line.  We've gone from a one-shot "selling my TV"
]to an *ongoing* for-profit enterprise.

How do you determine whether something is "for-profit"?  If someone pays 
herself a token amout for her time in assembling packages of products from
several sources as a desired service to people who want to buy the kits,
is that "for-profit"?  As a concrete example, I'd be pissed if the individuals
who sell robotics part kits were muzzled by their sites.  I buy such kits,
and the sellers are performing a service I consider valuable, and I think
the people who go to the trouble to assemble the kits ought to be able to 
compensate themselves for their time and effort.

A couple other "ongoing for-profit enterprises" that I think are justified
are postings which announce shareware uploaded to ftp sites, and adverts for
'zines published by the poster.  Are these to be banned, too?

In principle, I'd have to agree that people at educational sites shouldn't
be getting "free" advertizing at their Internet provider's expense.  However
it's very hard to draw the line between "compensation for a labor-of-love
project" and "shameless profiteering," and so I think sites should err on
the side of permissiveness.

]they've exchanged their status as a student
](or faculty, or staff) for that of a businessman.  Should we ex-
]pect to operate a business *and* enjoy academic protections at
]the same time?  I think not.

I think so, provided their ads don't significantly subsidize their incomes.
If the individuals are making 12 grand a year thanks to their Usenet posts,
you've got a point.  I think if a school finds such a person, they ought to
have the autority to say: pay for your access elsewhere, since you can 
obviously afford it--after proper due process and a thorough inquiry.  But
rules that expressly prohibit commercial activities (without "permission"
that is most unlikely to be granted), like the one you quote, are extreme
and unwarranted.

]Since I fall into the latter category, I feel that the policy I
]outlined above is a fair compromise.  Quite frankly, I don't be-
]lieve that Joe Student (or Jane Faculty, or Bill Staffmember) 
]should have a 'right' to conduct personal, *ongoing*, for-profit 
]business with our resources.  

I believe that they should, when the business involves something personal,
or is a valuable service that nets little profit.

]I will readily admit that the line I've drawn can be vague; however,
]the very nature of this issue makes the implementation of more spe-
]cific rules extremely difficult.  We don't want to throw the baby
](the occasional "selling my TV" posting) out with the bathwater 
](the "I'm setting up my CD distributorship on fing.edu" posting).

Exactly, the line is vague.  I find ads for robot kits, 'zines, and pirate
radio transmitter kits to be perfectly acceptable.

]In the particular case at hand, I will say that any suspension, or
]other sanction, should be approved by the appropriate authority.  I
]don't see how this could be classified as an emergency sanction (it's
]only Usenet, folks), so I would hope that the admins cleared the ac-
]tion with the proper authority.  

I'm glad you recognize due process.  I think that due process should include
a thorough investigation, and restriction should be based on a conclusion
that the effort is a money-making scheme only.  If the service is unique, and
desired by the targetted audience of the ad, I think it should be allowed.

