Christopher and I are in agreement about the more blatant advertisers,
but I do want to comment further on one or two things...

Christopher J Burian <cburian@ux4.cso.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>In principle, I'd have to agree that people at educational sites shouldn't
>be getting "free" advertizing at their Internet provider's expense.  However
>it's very hard to draw the line between "compensation for a labor-of-love
>project" and "shameless profiteering," and so I think sites should err on
>the side of permissiveness.

Actually, there's another problem - one more social than legal - to be
addressed.  Many state universities are prohibited from competing with
private industry.  While these restrictions primarily refer to direct
competition, does/should that prohibition extend to providing facilities 
to individual persons/businesses?

Think about this one.  There are (off the top of my head) 8 or 10 shops
in this town that sell trading cards (Topps/Upper Deck/etc.).  Access to
email certainly gives one/some of those people better access to *lots*
of people.  Should one of these businesses get a "leg up" just because
they take a class that gets them net access?  (This isn't as remote a
scenario as you may think - I was asked if I'd put a price list up on
our anonymous FTP server.)

Should the one Amway rep who takes CS 101 (and gets net access) have
blessing to solicit new folks for his "network marketing" via news/email,
at the (presumed) expense of those reps lacking access?

Quite frankly, the availability of net access actually *increases*
one's profit, even in smaller businesses like those you mention.  If
I get an emailbox through school, that translates into lots of order
forms, secretarial stuff and recordkeeping on which I *don't* have to
spend money.  Heck, they'll even make backups for you, right?  8)

>]they've exchanged their status as a student (or faculty, or staff) for 
>]that of a businessman.  Should we expect to operate a business *and* 
>]enjoy academic protections at the same time?  I think not.
>
>I think so, provided their ads don't significantly subsidize their incomes.

There's really no way to tell.  It isn't as if we call someone in and
ask for their income tax returns, eh?

>I believe that they should, when the business involves something personal,

As I've said, I have no problem with "selling my TV" messages, when placed
in appropriate forums.  You mention 'zines, kits, and a few other things;
I don't think most of us would complain about cost-recovery.  After all,
we're used to paying media charges for free software, right?

>or is a valuable service that nets little profit.

Heck, that's even more vague than my position, chief!

>]I will readily admit that the line I've drawn can be vague; however,
>]the very nature of this issue makes the implementation of more spe-
>]cific rules extremely difficult.  We don't want to throw the baby
>](the occasional "selling my TV" posting) out with the bathwater 
>](the "I'm setting up my CD distributorship on fing.edu" posting).
>
>Exactly, the line is vague.  I find ads for robot kits, 'zines, and pirate
>radio transmitter kits to be perfectly acceptable.

If we're talking cost-recovery, that's one thing - anything else is *way*
too vague.  I think that erring on the side of permissiveness, in this
case, will get us in trouble down the road.

Of course, I won't even ask if those 'zines were created in a school
facility.  I've had folks in the past running typing services, using
our public PC lab facilities.  Heck, yes, you can profit when you're
using someone else's PCs, software, and printers...why should this
be treated differently?

--Wes

