Even if the CP/M newsgroups were the biggest on the net and no one at a
university wanted them, the sex-related newsgroups would still be the first
to go.  We are dealing with humans here, not a utopian view of economics.
And humans will always target what they find offensive first.

> If you _really_ want them or any other group that badly, pay for the traffic
> and assume responsibility for the contents. No-one's stopping you.

So, pray tell: just what are the criteria for having a newsgroup in the
"standard feed" compared to having it in the "get it yourself feed"?  The
number of people who are interested in the topic?  If so, then the sex-related
newsgroups would be first on the list of newsgroups that all sites should
get and things like comp would be near the bottom.  There are a lot more
humans interested in sex than are interested in computers.  But, the
admins still target the sex-related newsgroups first.

And don't give me any crap about the comp groups being related to university
activity, but the sex newsgroups not being related.  All information that a
student or researcher would ever need about computers for their jobs or
courses is either in the library or is available on ftp/gopher/www archives.
Hence, there is no real need for comp newsgroups at all.  Heck, there's no
real need for any newsgroup for university activities.  We got on fine without
news for thousands of years.  Why not for a few thousand more?  It won't
take a genius of a VC to realise this and cut off _all_ news.  Then where
will you be?

> I, for one, don't want the groups in question (alt.sex, alt.binaries, etc.)
> and don't see any reason why anyone should indirectly and unnecessarily
> devote _my_ money to paying for your traffic by causing AARNet's costs to
> increase by carrying stuff I don't want nor that I have any need to convey
> to any other site.

There is a precedent for having everyone put in their bit.  It's the road
system, funded through taxation.  I never use the roads near your place, so
why the hell should I pay for them?  I therefore demand that the goverment
stop taxing me and only make me pay for the roads I use.  Yeah, right.
If we had to pay for the roads we use, it would cost us a lot more than
the tiny amount we pay on our taxes.

Say every directly-connected network paid $2000-$3000 for a news feed.  This
is not an unrealistic price according to my latest estimates which factor
in both machine and volume costs.  Every network would then be paying less
than a dollar per newsgroup.  That means that when you do want those
newsgroups, it costs you very little.  If however, the news feed is chopped
about, it will cost you a lot more per newsgroup that you want, and you'll
be paying more than $2000 for your news feed.

And this is beside the fact that the news software does not have any mechanism
for automatically requesting newsgroups.  If I want a newsgroup now, I have
to talk to my upstream site.  If he doesn't get it, he has to talk to his
upstream site, and so on.  As soon as it gets back to unimelb or wherever,
they say "we do not need such and such a newsgroup locally, so stuff
off because we didn't sign any contract with you to provide newsgroups
on demand".

We need a guaranteed news feed on the backbone so that if one site finds it
difficult to cope (for whatever reason), AARNet will actively co-ordinate
efforts to look for other ways to deliver the information.  People make noises
about how "if you want it, you can get it yourself and bypass the censors"
but the fact is that there is no _co-ordinated_ efforts to provide a
non-censorable news feed, so everyone suffers every time a person gets a
bee in their bonnet.

Cheers,

