Nobody seems to have said this clearly, although those who obviously 
understand the situation seem to have implied it in many postings.  As
I understand it, the following is a description of the way things are.
And, assuming my understanding is correct (not a foregone conclusion,
since I haven't read anybody's policy statements, I only know what's
technically possible) I'm really not sure what the problem is.

As someone said: "AARNet != USENET"

AARNet is a network, over which the connected institutions can transmit and
receive any traffic they want.  AARNet in no way restricts the content of
that traffic.  MU has decided it doesn't wish to continue to receive certain
newsgroups.  Since most international news comes via MU (in the sense that
most Australian sites are fed international news directly or indirectly by
MU) this means that distribution of these groups within Aus has "dried up".

To those who have complained, many have replied "arrange your own feed of 
these groups if you want them!"  To which the plaintifs reply "I can't
afford to / why should I pay for my own duplicate link to the US?"

But you *already* have a link to the US you are paying for -- it's called
AARNet.  You can use the existing network to transport your feed, nobody
is stopping you.  All you have to do is find an overseas internet site
prepared to provide that feed.  There's a good chance you can find someone
who will do this for nothing.  You don't need a new link.  And having
obtained the news, you in turn can feed it to whomever you like -- of course,
it still won't get to or through MU, due to local policy...

AARNet, of course, needs the money to provide the network which provides
the capacity which enables people to transmit and receive whatever they want.
Supposedly, the new charging model is "receiver pays" -- and this example
seems to me to be quite a good argument for this model.  kre has already
suggested what seems to me to be quite an effective solution to the problem
of distributing the initial (more expensive) costs of bringing international
news into Australia.

