note that the "Playboy book of humor", 1968 edition is in the University of
Melbourne's electronic library catalogue.  Someone please explain to me the
academic justification for this book.  And then explain to me why
sex-related humor (amongst other things) is banned on the computer system.

> In such a circumstance, what the hell makes you think that AARNet
> is going to take on the role of great amoral crusader for the right
> to publish and distribute anything you damn well please?

Then I suppose that Telecom is a great amoral crusader because it doesn't
actively police what people use its telephone lines for?  Get real.
Networks can't be moral or amoral.  Only the people using them can.
AARNet only needs to establish a "common carrier" principle of carrying
everything and anything with no value judgements on content, and only
the stupidest of lawyers will try to prosecute AARNet for "promoting
amorality".  They might as well prosecute Telecom for the same thing.

When problems occur on Telecom's network (e.g. over-explicit 0055 lines),
it is the user who goes down for any reprecussions.  When problems occur
on AARNet, it is AARNet's infrastructure that goes down.

> Just stop complaining when ITS and cs depts suddenly start waking
> up to the pointlessness of much of the alt hiearachy that takes
> up local cpu time and disk space.

I don't give a stuff what the ITS or cs departments do with their resources,
as long as the news feed _is_ available within the local region for other
organisations to access.  If you can't get the newsgroup you want in your
department, and you also can't get the newsgroup you want from the local
public access provider, then you're stuffed, period.

The cost of setting up a public access provider has now gone up from about
$5,000 p.a. (with pre-existing equipment) to about $100,000 p.a. or more.
So much for the little guy who has driven public access in this country up
until now.

If we don't co-operate on the news issue, it _will_ be the death of the net.
A little bit of sharing can go a long way.

Cheers,

Rhys.
