Grenville Armitage (gja@ee.mu.OZ.AU) wrote:

> What's with this "AANet hub" business? You mean AARNet fully owns a
> machine that uses AARNets own links to import and redistribute news?
> Or are you just being 'flexible' with your view that Uni == AARNet.

Maybe I'm playing a little fast and loose with terminology.  What I mean
by "AARNet hub" is the university or computer centre in each major region
which is responsible for looking after the AARNet routers and news feeds
for that region.

One correct interpretation of my comments would be to have an
AARNet-funded machine in each region which is used to import and
redistribute news.  Another is to have AARNet require the regional
providers to provide a minimum level of service for the benefit of
all of AARNet's users.  Each region can scramble all it wants to find
a way to provide that minimum service but unless AARNet makes it a
requirement, they won't bother.

> >Organisations like my own are dependent on the universities for helping to
> >share the costs of news feeds, but we have absolutely no say in what those
> >universities are required to provide. 

> "..required to provide"? This is getting too much, Rhys. When in the
> name of <insert favourite deity here> have Unis _ever_ been 'required'
> to support every Tom, Dick, and Harry BBS that wants a full news feed?

Exactly.  The unis are not required to provide anything, so as soon as
volume charging comes along, news _will_ dry up unless we find a solution,
and soon.

> 	[..]
> >Research material on sex issues can be justified, but it's interesting to
> >note that the "Playboy book of humor", 1968 edition is in the University of
> >Melbourne's electronic library catalogue.  Someone please explain to me the
> >academic justification for this book.  And then explain to me why
> >sex-related humor (amongst other things) is banned on the computer system.

> Who knows, eh? But let me guess... the library doesn't run a newshost
> at MU (yet), and no one has (calmly) suggested to the VCs that this
> might be worth pursuing?

Even if it was worth pursuing, what's the difference between shelling out
money for the computer centre to run a news host and the library to run
a news host?  Both the same cost, and probably both done by exactly the same
people.  Do you know any librarians with Unix admin experience?  It will
be the computer centre that will operate it.

> But I suppose this answer doesn't involve your bogey-men of
> big, bad, Geoff and AARNet, so it can't be valid, eh?

The only thing big and bad about them is that they are completely unwilling
to discuss these issues in the open and try to find a solution of benefit
to everyone.  I am advocating discussion, but you and others seem to stick
to "we don't have to discuss it with you so stuff off Rhys".

Heck, if I could set up the news distribution network necessary, I'd do it,
but it is beyond the capabilities of my organisation.  I therefore am
reliant on the universities and am more than willing to pay my fair share.
But, since they are not required to listen to me, I am being hamstrung
in my efforts.  If the local "hub" (see wording above) drops alt, I'm
basically stuffed.  I cannot afford to import it myself, but the extra
$100-$200 a year for alt is chicken feed to all the feeds in my region.

> >AARNet only needs to establish a "common carrier" principle of carrying
> >everything and anything with no value judgements on content, and only
> >the stupidest of lawyers will try to prosecute AARNet for "promoting
> >amorality".  They might as well prosecute Telecom for the same thing.

> Why do I get the feeling you've got 'Huston' in your Killfile? I saw
> Geoff post recently to the effect that AARNet has no way of being
> legally considered a 'common carrier'. But, be that as it may, your
> analogy with Telecom is naieve (at best) and stupid (at worst) anyway.

The principle is what is important.  Networks such as uunet and netcom.com
in the US provide extensive news services and also have a policy of not
censoring anything.  That gives them extra protections.  Not quite the
same as a real common carrier, but enough so they don't get sued by the
first idiot to get a bee in his/her bonnet about something transmitted
using their links.  Maybe the name isn't legally correct, but the principle
is what counts.

> What on earth do you mean by this? AARNets infrastructure is a
> network. It has users (such as Melbourne Uni) who choose to utilise
> this network as they see fit. In one sense the Unis _are_ the 0055
> providers of AARNet. And (once more, with feeling...) 
> Internet != Usenet != AARNet != Unis (!= connect.com too, for completeness)

You don't get it do you?  I want there to be open discussion to try and find
a solution that will benefit everyone.

> 	[..]
> >The cost of setting up a public access provider has now gone up from about
> >$5,000 p.a. (with pre-existing equipment) to about $100,000 p.a. or more.
> >So much for the little guy who has driven public access in this country up
> >until now.

> I'm tempted to say "so what?". Your sales pitch to the Unis has been
> a never ending tirade demanding vaguely justified "rights" to usenet
> news flow. Poor old "little guy who has driven public access". This
> little guy has been benefitting from the GOODWILL of the larger
> organisations directly on AARNet - its not a 'right'.

And this goodwill is now dead.  So we have to find another way to provide
the same service or risk losing it altogether.

Maybe I'm cutting my legs off here with my tirades, but I am not going to
shut up.  We must find a viable long-term solution.  Attacking me won't
find a solution.  Coming up with suggestions will.  I've come up with some
that you think to be flawed, so come up with alternatives.  Debate my
figures and my proposals, not my attitude.

Another hobby horse of mine: no one, least of the all the universities and
AARNet, have produced any real figures that prove that public access has had
a "free ride".  If they prove it, we can negotiate on ways to address the
situation.  But, they've all fallen back to "Under the companies code, we
are not required to talk to you since you aren't one of our owners.  Nick off!"

> 	[..]
> >A little bit of sharing can go a long way.

> Ah, yes. Rhys style - "Dammit, you HAVE to share with me!"

Dammit Grenville.  Add up the costs yourself.  It is a lot of bloody money,
and beyond the capability of any one single organisation to handle.  But,
no one in AARNet or the unversities is currently interested in trying to find
a viable long-term cost-sharing solution.  Everyone has clammed up with
"Fuck off Rhys, we're not going to tell you anything".  Sharing is dead,
for a medium that requires sharing to operate effectively.

